    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 042300\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n400 PM MST Fri Sep 4 2026\n\n.UPDATE...\nUpdated Aviation\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Residual moisture may spark a few showers and storms this \nafternoon, mainly south and east of Phoenix.\n\n- The moisture axis and corresponding rain chances will shift west \ninto southeast California and southwest Arizona during the weekend. \n\n- Temperatures will remain near to slightly below normal through \nthis weekend before warming to slightly above normal by early next \nweek. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nThe overall synoptic pattern continues to be defined by an elongated \nridge of high pressure extending from the Southern Plains into the \nsoutheastern U.S. while a broad trough encompasses much of the \nPacific Northwest and west coast. Due to this pattern configuration, \nthe Desert Southwest remains under dry southwesterly flow aloft. \nResidual low-level moisture from Hurricane Marie, centered well to \nthe west of the southern tip of the Baja Peninsula, remains across \nmuch south-central and southeast AZ with dewpoints in the 60s while \nmuch drier air with dewpoints in the 30s and 40s encompasses much of \nsoutheast CA and southwest AZ. The residual moisture will still \ncombine with strong daytime heating to cause cumulus buildups \nheading into this afternoon across south-central AZ with the \npotential for a few showers and storms to develop, mainly across the \nterrain escarpments. However, as the afternoon progresses, drier air \nwill be filtering in from the west, which will erode the low-level \nmoisture somewhat and thus reduce instability significantly with the \nlatest forecast soundings from Phoenix showing CAPE values dropping \nfrom over 1000 J/KG earlier today to less than 100-200 J/KG by this \nafternoon. Therefore, any convective cells that attempt to develop \nthis afternoon will be very short-lived. \n\nAs Hurricane Marie continues to move northwestward well offshore \nfrom the Baja Peninsula over the weekend, some additional moisture \nwill be transported northward but this time towards southern CA and \nsouthwest AZ as PWATs rise to around 1.6-1.8\". Therefore, rain \nchances heading into this weekend will be mainly confined across \nthese areas with PoPs only topping out between 10-20%. An abundance \nof mid to upper-level moisture will result in extensive mid-high \nclouds across much of the area so as a result mid-level lapse rates \nand thus instability will be very poor with forecast soundings \nshowing little to no CAPE. As a result, any rainfall activity that \nmaterializes will be on the light side with little to no impacts. In \nfact, much of the activity that falls on Saturday across southeast \nCA and southwest AZ may actually be virga given that the low- levels \nwill remain very dry as dewpoints will still be in the 30s and 40s. \nBetter low-level moisture is expected heading into Sunday, however, \nthe better forcing for ascent from an upper- level jet streak is \nlikely to shift northward towards the Mohave Desert Region, where \nthe best rain chances will be located. Fairly quiet weather is \nexpected this weekend across much of south- central AZ as the lack \nof instability and forcing for ascent will hinder storm activity. \n\nThe continued presence of troughing to our west will continue to \nmaintain temperatures near to slightly below normal levels through \nthe weekend with highs generally in the upper 90s to low 100s, with \nSaturday expected to be the coolest day. In fact, due to the \npresence of abundant mid to high clouds across much of the western \ndeserts on Saturday, temperatures may struggle to reach the triple \ndigits. The associated height falls from the troughing to our west \nwill also result in another round of afternoon/early evening \nbreeziness today across much of southeast CA and southwest AZ with \npeak gusts around 20-30 mph, potentially reaching up to 40 mph in \nthe typical wind prone areas of far southwest Imperial County. \nSlightly weaker winds are anticipated during the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/... \nDeterministic and ensemble guidance continue to differ on the \nmagnitude and positioning of the subtropical ridge early next week \nwhich will dictate how hot temperatures get and how soon rain \nchances will return to our area. Both the EPS and GEFS show the \nridge strengthening over the Southern Plains through the middle of \nthe week which will allow hghts aloft to increase across much of the \nDesert Southwest. In turn, temperatures will also increase from near \nnormal Monday to slightly above normal on Tuesday. The temperature \nforecast becomes much more unclear heading into the middle of next \nweek with large spreads in the NBM interquartile range. This is \nlikely due to differences in the magnitude of the ridge with many \nEPS members showing a stronger ridge with 500 mb hghts approaching \n596-598 dam while the GEFS shows slightly lower overall hghts. For \nnow NBM temperatures show a cooling trend after Tuesday which is \nlikely due to an increase in moisture as southeasterly flow aloft \nreturns to the lower deserts. \n\nPWATs are expected to increase back to around 1.5-1.8\" by the middle \nof next week which will bring rain chances back into the picture by \nthe Wednesday-Thursday timeframe when PoPs increase to around 20-40% \nareawide. The GFS is even showing an easterly wave/inverted trough \narriving into southern AZ along with additional tropical Pacific \nmoisture on Wednesday which could boost rain chances significantly, \nbut we will likely have to wait for more run to run consistency as \nthese features are always difficult to resolve at longer time \nscales. Either way, most of the GEFS and EPS members are showing \nmeasurable precipitation across portions of the area by the middle \nof next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 2300Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nOther than a remote chance for an isold SHRA/TSRA early this \nevening, winds will be the only weather issue through Saturday \nevening under frequent mid/high cigs. Minimal 10% chances of a \nSHRA/TSRA impacting a Phoenix terminal exists through mid evening \nbefore abating by midnight with good confidence of winds finally \nsettling on a W/SW direction. Confidence is also good that the \ntypical nocturnal switch to east will occur later than usual well \nafter midnight. Any afternoon gustiness should be limited. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nGusty late afternoon/early evening winds will be the main weather \nimpact through Saturday evening under thickening mid/high clouds. \nW/SW gusts 20-25kt will be common at both locations before speeds \nrelax overnight, and even becoming light and variable into the \nmorning. Virga/isold SHRA will be common Saturday afternoon, though \nshould yield minimal operational impacts. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nResidual moisture may spark a few showers and storms this afternoon \nacross south-central AZ. Rain chances will expand westward through \nthis weekend, becoming focused over the western districts Saturday \nand Sunday. However, the chances for wetting rains will remain very \nlow at less than 10%. Afternoon minimum humidities will range from 7-\n15% across southeast CA and between 20-35% for the the AZ deserts \ntoday and will likely increase above 20-25% areawide starting this \nweekend. Overnight recoveries will range from poor across the \nwestern deserts to good across the eastern districts tonight and \nincrease to good areawide this weekend. Winds will remain elevated \nout of the south to southwest, with the strongest gusts in Southeast \nCA and along the Lower Colorado River Valley peaking between 20-30 \nmph this afternoon/early evening. Lighter winds, generally around 15 \nmph or less will transpire this weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Lojero\nLONG TERM...Salerno \nAVIATION...Benedict \nFIRE WEATHER...Salerno/Lojero\n",
